            <ArticleTitle>Open randomised trial of intermittent very low energy diet together with nicotine gum for stopping smoking in women who gained weight in previous attempts to quit.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>490-3; discussion 494</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine whether attempts to prevent weight gain will increase success rates for stopping smoking.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">16 week, open, randomised study with 1 year follow up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Obesity unit.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">287 female smokers who had quit smoking before but started again because of weight concerns.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Combination of a standard smoking cessation programme with nicotine gum and a behavioural weight control programme including a very low energy diet. A control group was treated with the identical programme but without the diet.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sustained cessation of smoking.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After 16 weeks, 68/137 (50%) women had stopped smoking in the diet group versus 53/150 (35%) in the control group (P=0.01). Among these women, weight fell by mean 2.1 (95% confidence interval 2.9 to 1.3) kg in the diet group but increased by 1.6 (0.9 to 2.3) kg in the control group (P&lt;0.001). After 1 year the success rates in the diet and control groups were 38/137 (28%) and 24/150 (16%) respectively (P&lt;0.05), but there was no statistical difference in weight gain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Combining the smoking cessation programme with an intervention to control weight helped women to stop smoking and control weight.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
